1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,440
Why does cola
taste better

2
00:00:01,440 --> 00:00:02,880
from a glass bottle?

3
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:04,180
The recipe's the same.

4
00:00:04,180 --> 00:00:05,840
What changes
is the bottle.

5
00:00:05,840 --> 00:00:06,660
A bottle of cola has

6
00:00:06,660 --> 00:00:08,120
four times
its own volume

7
00:00:08,120 --> 00:00:10,180
of gas squeezed
into it.

8
00:00:10,180 --> 00:00:11,100
Plastic looks solid,

9
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:12,180
but to this gas,

10
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:13,000
it's a sieve.

11
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:13,840
The bubbles seep

12
00:00:13,840 --> 00:00:14,720
through the wall,

13
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:16,300
about two percent
every week.

14
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:17,800
Cola that's lost
a sixth of its gas

15
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:19,020
counts as flat,

16
00:00:19,020 --> 00:00:20,300
even if it was
never opened.

17
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:21,680
That gas turns
into acid

18
00:00:21,680 --> 00:00:22,560
on your tongue.

19
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:23,990
That's the
bite you love.

20
00:00:24,660 --> 00:00:25,940
A can doesn't
let the gas out,

21
00:00:25,940 --> 00:00:26,980
but the inside is lined

22
00:00:26,980 --> 00:00:28,960
with a thin
layer of plastic.

23
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:29,780
In one experiment,

24
00:00:29,780 --> 00:00:31,160
this lining
absorbed almost all

25
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:32,960
of the aromas
in two weeks.

26
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:33,800
Glass, on the
other hand,

27
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:36,010
lets nothing out
and absorbs nothing.

28
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:37,280
So cola in glass

29
00:00:37,280 --> 00:00:38,930
doesn't taste better...

30
00:00:39,260 --> 00:00:40,100
it's just exactly

31
00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:41,530
how it left the factory.
